<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!-- Untracked per-machine game locations; see local.props.example. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)local.props" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)local.props')" />
  <PropertyGroup Label="UserMacros">
    <GAMENAME>Klingon</GAMENAME>
    <GAMEDIR>$(GAMEDIR_KLINGON)</GAMEDIR>
    <!--
      Left empty, as for Nerf: the SDK under Games\Klingon carries Editor, Fire, WinDrv, Window
      and GlideDrv libraries but no game executable, so there is nothing here to derive it from.
      Only GAMENAME and GAMEDIR are consumed, by Render DLL.props, so an empty value costs
      nothing. A wrong one would reach deploy.ps1's safety check and make it refuse a correct
      install.

      TODO: fill in once confirmed against a real installation.
    -->
    <GAMEEXE></GAMEEXE>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_ProjectFileVersion>10.0.30319.1</_ProjectFileVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    The one game here built ANSI, not Unicode, and it has to be: Games\Klingon's prebuilt Core.lib
    exports entry points taking char const* where every other SDK here exports the wide form
    (dumpbin shows PBD against their PBG), and its Core.h selects TCHAR from _UNICODE just as theirs
    do. Asking for Unicode sends the linker looking for wide symbols the library does not contain:
    a clean compile followed by two dozen unresolved externals.

    The setting lives on the two Klingon configurations in d3d10drv.vcxproj, not here.
    Microsoft.Cpp.props turns CharacterSet into the UNICODE and _UNICODE defines and is imported
    before any of these sheets, so a CharacterSet set here would be read after the decision it
    feeds and silently do nothing.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <!--
        Engine 219, the oldest tree here. Its render interface is the Unreal Gold 226 one and older
        still, so KLINGON sets the OLD_URENDERDEVICE umbrella in d3d10drv.h: Flush takes no argument,
        Exec is pure virtual so nothing may chain to it, DECLARE_CLASS takes the three-argument form
        and there is no URenderDevice::DetailTextures.

        Beyond that umbrella it needs a define of its own. Its URenderDevice stops at EndFlash()
        and carries only Viewport and eight capability bitfields, so there is no SupportsTC to
        negotiate texture compression through - compression is forced off, not merely defaulted
        off - and no SetSceneNode, PrecacheTexture, Draw3DLine, Draw2DClippedLine or DrawStats
        slot. Its FTextureInfo has no Texture pointer and packs the realtime flags into a
        TextureFlags DWORD. Its engine calls StaticConstructor as a plain function taking the
        UClass, not as a method on the class default object.
      -->
      <PreprocessorDefinitions>KLINGON;%(PreprocessorDefinitions)</PreprocessorDefinitions>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMENAME">
      <Value>$(GAMENAME)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMEDIR">
      <Value>$(GAMEDIR)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
    <BuildMacro Include="GAMEEXE">
      <Value>$(GAMEEXE)</Value>
    </BuildMacro>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
